text:
In the binary treatment setting with known propensity score \(\pi(X)=p(Z=1\mid X)\), Zhao et al. proposed outcome weighted learning (OWL), which reformulates value maximization as an inverse-probability-weighted objective based only on observed data, contrasting with Zhang’s formulation based on the unobserved treatment contrast \(\Delta(X)\). Thus, OWL can be viewed as an observed-data, implementable counterpart to Zhang’s broader weighted-classification characterization of the optimal rule. In its original form, however, OWL has several important restrictions. It is developed for binary treatments, assumes the treatment mechanism is known or consistently estimated with positivity \(0&lt;\pi(X)&lt;1\), and requires the reward used as the classification weight to be nonnegative. The nonnegativity is needed for the weighted SVM interpretation because the case weights \(Y_i/p(A_i\mid X_i)\) must be nonnegative and therefore to apply OWL \(Y_i \geq 0\) (a rather restrictive requirement).
OWL is built from the IPW value,
\[
\begin{aligned}
d^{opt}
&\in \arg\max_{d:\mathcal X\to\{-1,1\}} V(d) \\
&= \arg\max_{d:\mathcal X\to\{-1,1\}}
\mathbb{E}\left[
\frac{\mathbb{1}\{Z=d(X)\}}{p(Z\mid X)}Y
\right] \\
&= \arg\max_{d:\mathcal X\to\{-1,1\}}
\left\{
\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{Y}{p(Z\mid X)}\right]
-
\mathbb{E}\left[
\frac{Y}{p(Z\mid X)}
\mathbb{1}\{Z\neq d(X)\}
\right]
\right\}.
\end{aligned}
\] Since the first term does not depend on \(d\), maximizing the value is equivalent to minimizing \[
d^{opt}
\in
\arg\min_{d:\mathcal X\to\{-1,1\}}
\mathbb{E}\left[
\frac{Y}{p(Z\mid X)}
\mathbb{1}\{Z\neq d(X)\}
\right].
\]
Usually given treatments are coded \(-1/1\) we have, \[
d^{opt}
\in
\arg\min_{d:\mathcal X\to\{-1,1\}}
\mathbb{E}\left[
\frac{Y}{Z\pi(X) - (1-Z)/2}
\mathbb{1}\{Z\neq d(X)\}
\right].
\] in an RCT,
Usually given treatments are coded \(-1/1\) we have, \[
d^{opt}
\in
\arg\min_{d:\mathcal X\to\{-1,1\}}
\mathbb{E}\left[
\frac{Y}{Z\pi - (1-Z)/2}
\mathbb{1}\{Z\neq d(X)\}
\right].
\] where \(\pi = p(A=1)\)
This objective depends only on observed data and does not require first estimating \(\Delta(X)\). However, the \(0\)–\(1\) loss remains discontinuous and therefore it is difficult to optimize directly. Zhao et al. proposed replacing it with a convex surrogate of Hinge loss,


Warning: package 'ggplot2' was built under R version 4.4.3









Using the hinge loss, OWL estimates a real-valued surrogate decision function by minimizing the weighted hinge-risk \[
\tilde d^{\mathrm{opt}}
\in
\arg\min_d
\mathbb{E}\left[
\frac{Y}{p(A\mid X)}\phi\{A d(X)\}
\right],
\qquad
\phi(t)=(1-t)^+,
\] where \(d:\mathcal X\to\mathbb R\). The corresponding treatment rule is induced by its sign, \[
\operatorname{sign}\{\tilde d^{\mathrm{opt}}(X)\}.
\] In practice, OWL solves the regularized empirical problem and is referred to as \(\ell_2\) Penalized OWL (POWL) \[
\hat d
\in
\arg\min_d
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n
\frac{Y_i}{p(A_i\mid X_i)}
\max\{1-A_i d(X_i),0\}
+\lambda\|d\|^2,
\] where \(\lambda&gt;0\) controls the complexity of the decision function. The estimated treatment rule is then \[
\operatorname{sign}\{\hat d(X)\}.
\] This is essentially a soft-margin SVM objective with case weights \(Y_i/p(A_i\mid X_i)\), so OWL can be implemented using weighted SVM machinery and naturally accommodates kernel methods for nonlinear decision boundaries. The key justification for replacing the original weighted \(0\)–\(1\) loss by this convex surrogate is Fisher consistency, stated formally below.

Core Ideas: Frequentist Maximum Likelihood Estimation and Likelihood based/Model based inference/hyptohesis testing

