“A set is a many that allows itself to be thought of as a one.” — Georg Cantor


A set is a collection of objects, called its elements or members. A set is determined entirely by which elements belong to it: neither their order nor repetition matters. For example, the sets of integers \(\{2,4,6\}\) and \(\{6,2,4,2\}\) are equal, since they contain exactly the same elements.

Membership relates an object to a set. We write \(x\in A\) to say that \(x\) is an element of \(A\).
Inclusion relates one set to another. We write \(A\subset B\) when every element of \(A\) also belongs to \(B\). We call \(A\) a subset of \(B\) and \(B\) a superset of \(A\). We can write the same relationship as \(B\supset A\). Generally if \(x\in A\) then \(\{x\} \subset A\).







Theorem: Set Equality



Two sets are equal, written \(A=B\), if and only if they contain exactly the same elements. Equivalently,
\[
A=B \iff A\subseteq B \text{ and } B\subseteq A.
\]








Remark: Ways to show set equality





Proving set equality by establishing both inclusions is called double inclusion. Each inclusion can be proved directly or by contraposition. For example, to show \(A\subseteq B\), we may instead show that \(x\notin B\) implies \(x\notin A\).
If \(A\) and \(B\) are finite, it suffices to establish one inclusion together with equality of cardinalities:
\[
A\subseteq B \quad \text{and} \quad |A|=|B|
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad A=B.
\]









Example: Set Equality





Let \(A\) be the set of integers that leave a remainder of \(1\) when divided by \(4\). We can describe its elements using set-builder notation:
\[
A=\{4k+1:k\in\mathbb Z\}.
\]
Here \(\mathbb Z\) denotes the integers. Read the expression as “all numbers of the form \(4k+1\) as \(k\) ranges over the integers.”
Let \(B\) be the set of integers that are one less than twice an odd integer:
\[
B=\{2m-1:m\in\mathbb Z,\ m\text{ is odd}\}.
\]
Do these descriptions pick out the same integers?

Show \(A\subset B\). If \(n\in A\), then \(n=4k+1\) for some integer \(k\). Rewrite this as \(n=2(2k+1)-1\). Since \(2k+1\) is odd, \(n\) is one less than twice an odd integer. Thus \(n\in B\).
Show \(B\subset A\). If \(n\in B\), then \(n=2m-1\) for some odd integer \(m\). Write \(m=2k+1\) for some integer \(k\). Substituting gives \(n=2(2k+1)-1=4k+1\), so \(n\in A\).

Both inclusions hold, so \(A=B\).









Remark: Subset Notation





Every set is a subset of itself and may also be a subset of larger sets. Throughout these notes, we write \(A\subset B\) to mean that every element of \(A\) also belongs to \(B\), including the possibility that \(A=B\).
Some authors reserve \(\subset\) for proper subsets, where \(A\neq B\), and use \(\subseteq\) when equality is allowed. We use \(\subset\) in the more general sense throughout.





A sample space, denoted \(\Omega\), is the set of all possible outcomes of a random process generating uncertain outcomes. We use \(\omega\) for an individual outcome. An event \(A\) is a subset of the sample space. It occurs when the observed outcome belongs to it, that is, when \(\omega\in A\). We can describe an event by listing its outcomes or by giving a condition for membership.
\[
A=\{\omega\in\Omega:\omega\text{ satisfies the condition}\}.
\]






Example: Drawing a card





If we draw one card and record only its rank, our sample space is
\[
\Omega=\{2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10,\text{Jack},\text{Queen},\text{King},\text{Ace}\}.
\]
That is, although a standard deck contains four twos, we list the rank only once in this set of ranks.

The element \(2\) is an outcome, so \(2\in\Omega\).
The singleton set \(\{2\}\) is an event so \(\{2\}\subset\Omega\).

We can specify a membership condition to define a (sub)set,
\[
\{\omega\in\Omega:\omega\text{ is a face-card}\}
=\{\text{Jack},\text{Queen},\text{King}, \text{Ace}\}.
\]
We can also choose elements and form a (sub)set directly, \[\{\text{Jack},9,\text{King},2,\text{Ace}\}\]






Each set operation gives a rule for deciding which elements belong to the resulting set. In the expressions below, read the colon as “such that,”
\[
\begin{align}
A \cup B
&\coloneqq \{x \in \Omega : x \in A \text{ or } x \in B\}
\tag{Union}\\
A \cap B
&= AB \coloneqq \{x \in \Omega : x \in A \text{ and } x \in B\}
\tag{Intersection}\\
A^c
&= \Omega - A = \Omega \setminus A
\coloneqq \{x \in \Omega : x \notin A\}
\tag{Complement}\\
A \setminus B
&= A - B \coloneqq \{x \in A : x \notin B\}
\tag{Difference}\\
A \triangle B
&\coloneqq (A \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A)
= (A \cup B) \setminus (A \cap B)
\tag{Symmetric difference}
\end{align}
\]
The union is inclusive meaning it contains elements that belong to at least one set while the symmetric difference is exclusive containing elements belonging to exactly one set

