Think of laying out a sequence of \(m\) “stars” and \(n-1\) “bars.” The first block of stars (before the first bar) counts how many of type 1 we chose, the next block (between bar 1 and bar 2) how many of type 2, and so on, up through type \(n\) (after the last bar). In total there are \[
m\text{ stars} + (n-1)\text{ bars} = m + n - 1
\] positions, and we need only choose which \(m\) of those are stars. Hence \[
\binom{m + n - 1}{m}
\]






