Tip: Stars and Bars Can Be Used to Count Solutions to linear Diophantine equations





The stars-and-bars method can be used to count the number of nonnegative integer solutions to equations of the form
\[
x_1+x_2+\cdots+x_n=m,
\qquad x_j\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq0}
\]
There are \(m\) identical units (stars) distributed among \(n\) variables, separated by \(n-1\) bars. Since the arrangement contains \(m+n-1\) positions, choosing the positions of the bars gives
\[
\binom{m+n-1}{n-1}
\]
Inequality constraints can also be handled by introducing a nonnegative slack variable. For example, to count solutions to
\[
x_1+\cdots+x_n\leq m,
\]
define \(x_{n+1}=m-\sum_{j=1}^n x_j\geq0\). This gives the equivalent equation
\[
x_1+\cdots+x_n+x_{n+1}=m.
\]
We now have \(n+1\) nonnegative variables, so the number of solutions is
\[
\binom{m+n}{n}
\]
These formulas assume the variables are nonnegative integers without individual upper bounds. If constraints such as \(x_j\leq b_j\) are imposed, stars and bars may count invalid solutions, which must be excluded, often using the inclusion–exclusion principle.









Example: Distributing Halloween candies





Suppose a group of \(n=3\) children arrives at your door on Halloween asking for candy. How many ways can we distribute \(m=6\) identical candies among these \(n=3\) children, allowing some children to receive none?
Let \(x_i\) denote the number of candies received by child \(i\). We want to count the nonnegative integer solutions to \[
x_1+\cdots+x_n=m.
\]
Represent each candy by a star and use \(n-1\) bars to partition the stars into \(n\) groups, one for each child. There are \(m+n-1\) slots in total, and choosing which \(n-1\) contain bars determines the allocation. Thus, the number of possible allocations is \[
\binom{m+n-1}{n-1}
=\binom{6+3-1}{3-1}
=\binom{8}{2}
=28.
\]
Notice that adjacent bars represent an empty group. For example, ***||*** corresponds to \((3,0,3)\), where the second child receives no candies.






When selecting \(i\) items from \(n\) distinct items without replacement and without regard to order, we can assign each item one of two labels: selected or not selected. There are \(i\) repetitions of the first label and \(n-i\) repetitions of the second. Counting the distinct arrangements of these labels gives
\[
\binom{n}{i}
=
\frac{n!}{i!(n-i)!}
=
\binom{n}{i,n-i}.
\]
The multinomial coefficient generalizes this idea from two categories to \(K\) categories.






Theorem: Multinomial Coefficient (Thm. 1.4, Rozanov)



Suppose we have \(n\) distinct elements and \(K\) labeled categories. Let \(n_1,\ldots,n_K\) be nonnegative integers satisfying \(\sum_{i=1}^K n_i=n\). The number of ways to partition the elements into these categories, with exactly \(n_i\) elements in category \(i\), is
\[
P(n;n_1,\ldots,n_K)
\coloneqq
\binom{n}{n_1,\ldots,n_K}
=
\frac{n!}{n_1!\cdots n_K!}.
\tag{15}\]

