Associational and Causal Estimands

Causal effects/contrasts






Definition: Average Causal Effects



Assume \(E[|Y(a)|]&lt;\infty\) for \(a=0,1\) and \(0&lt;P(A=1)&lt;1\). We can define causal estimands using expectations of the potential outcomes:








Quantity
Potential-outcomes representation
Interpretation




ATE
\(E[Y(1)-Y(0)]\)
Average effect in the population


ATT
\(E[Y(1)-Y(0)\mid A=1]\)
Average effect among those treated


ATU
\(E[Y(1)-Y(0)\mid A=0]\)
Average effect among those untreated


CATE
\(\tau(x)=E[Y(1)-Y(0)\mid X=x]\)
Average effect in the subgroup with covariates \(x\)











Remark: PATE and SATE















Remark: ITE vs CATE



We have to be careful to distinguish the individual effect \(\tau_i=Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)\) and the conditional average treatment effect: \[
\tau(x)=E[\tau \mid X=x] = E[Y(1)-Y(0)\mid X=x].
\]
By iterated expectation, averaging the CATE over the target population’s covariate distribution gives the average treatment effect: \[
ATE=E[\tau]
=E_X\!\left[E[\tau\mid X]\right]
=E_X[\tau(X)].
\]
The CATE averages individual effects among people with covariates \(X=x\). Identifying \(\tau(x)\) does not generally identify those individual effects or the joint distribution of \((Y(0),Y(1))\).




Observed-data contrasts
We know we cannot directly observe all the potential outcomes needed for these contrasts (at least in full generality, there are certainly systems where we can argue this is possible, namely tightly controlled experiments in basic natural sciences etc.). Instead, we have to work with what we actually have the realized or observed data.






Definition: Observed-Data Contrasts



Assume \(E[|Y|\mid A=a]&lt;\infty\) for \(a=0,1\) and \(0&lt;P(A=1)&lt;1\). The following contrasts compare observed outcomes across treatment groups. The subscript \(\mathrm{obs}\) distinguishes them from causal estimands defined using potential outcomes.








Quantity
Observed-data representation
Interpretation




\(ATE_{\mathrm{obs}}\)
\(E[Y\mid A=1]-E[Y\mid A=0]\)
Observed mean difference between treated and untreated groups


\(ATT_{\mathrm{obs}}\)
No direct within-treated analogue
Within the treated group, there are no untreated observations for comparison


\(ATU_{\mathrm{obs}}\)
No direct within-untreated analogue
Within the untreated group, there are no treated observations for comparison


\(CATE_{\mathrm{obs}}(x)\)
\(E[Y\mid A=1,X=x]-E[Y\mid A=0,X=x]\)
Observed conditional mean difference, defined almost everywhere on the common covariate support



The \(\mathrm{obs}\) notation is a descriptive convention, not a claim that these contrasts identify causal effects. ATT and ATU can still be identified through adjusted comparisons under appropriate assumptions; the corresponding rows indicate only that a direct within-group treated-minus-untreated comparison is unavailable.



